## Daily commands

```bash
just prove      # fast property closure + Hypothesis + bounded CrossHair
just prove-one ITEM-ID-VALID  # shortest loop for one changed property
just check      # deterministic repairs followed by the full acceptance gate
just prove-deep # larger generated/stateful/symbolic search for risky changes
just proof-report  # print the property/evidence matrix
just doctor     # fast deployment and publication readiness verdict
just session-log <transcript> [output] [agent]  # preserve Claude/Codex evidence
just scaffold-update  # merge the newest tagged scaffold release
just update     # update dependencies and hook revisions intentionally
```

`architecture.toml` is the single ownership map for the repository. Every path
belongs to exactly one of FOUNDATION, PRODUCT, DERIVED, or DECLARATION.
`.repo/repository.toml` is the versioned repository declaration; generated
indexes under `src/{{ package }}/_generated/` and `proof/_generated/` carry a
source-state digest and may be replaced. PRODUCT roots are user-owned: the
scaffold contains no files there, and scaffold updates must leave existing
product bytes unchanged. `just check` rejects overlapping or unclassified paths
and applies the same required structure, dependency direction, and `api.py`
public-surface rules to system and product capabilities (ADR-0007).
The draft `repoctl.modules.repository_generation` system capability ships as a
hexagonal capsule under FOUNDATION ownership. Its immutable intent, snapshot,
operation, and plan values have icontract invariants, canonical JSON, and a
content-derived plan ID. Its pure derived-index compiler projects exactly the
active declarations into canonical bytes under
`REPOCTL::DERIVED-INDEX-EXACT`. Its pure path classifier is the sole
interpreter of the ownership roots; `scripts/ownership.py` only adapts
filesystem-shaped values. Absolute paths, parent escapes, empty segments, and
non-canonical Unicode fail with named boundary errors, and
`REPOCTL::PLAN-PATH-CLOSED` links every successful classification to an
independent oracle, Hypothesis, and CrossHair. Its pure
`plan(snapshot, intent)` decision takes ownership roots and prior file digests
as explicit snapshot data, creates only absent PRODUCT seeds, and emits every
declaration and derived write with an exact precondition. Canonical bytes and
the plan ID are deterministic under `REPOCTL::PLAN-DETERMINISTIC`, independent
of CWD, environment variables, and snapshot enumeration order. The capsule
still has no placeholder product model, lifecycle transition, or effectful
application behavior.

The proof loop is intentionally narrower than the full gate. One
`proof/policy.toml` sets the shared discovery policy and names the ownership-zone
catalogs that close the public domain surface. Each critical symbol is declared
in its owning catalog with a falsifiable property, exact scope, external
assumptions, independent pure oracle, `icontract` link, canonical Hypothesis
proof, and one minimal canary per declared oracle. The loader aggregates every
catalog into one proof index. The guard resolves exact production and oracle
imports, rejects constructor-only evidence for a targeted method, and confines
stateful evidence to the literal `RuleBasedStateMachine` executed by the
canonical test. It also rejects effect-shaped oracles and oracles that import or
call the behavior they judge. Proof evidence may import a capability's stable
`api.py`; the guard follows direct re-exports back to the exact defining target
and oracle. Every contracted public core function or method
must be an explicit CrossHair target, so agents cannot skip symbolic evidence,
and that symbolic run must refute a deliberately false canary contract before its
silence counts as a result. Source and verification roots are declared once in
the policy: CrossHair builds its import path from those roots, and the canonical
`verification/tests/` collection imports proof evidence from
`verification/repoctl/`. The daily loop searches meaningful state without
traversing CLI, database, GitHub, MCP, or network effects. The full recipe and
evidence rules are in
`docs/architecture/PROVABILITY.md` (ADR-0006).

Run `just doctor` immediately before deployment or publication. It emits one
stable `ok`, `warn`, or `fail` line for hooks, the working tree, default-branch
synchronization with `origin`, GitHub CLI authentication, dependency
synchronization, and Python version, followed by a verdict. Missing remotes,
an absent or offline GitHub CLI, and policy intentionally omitted by the
selected configuration are warnings; any failed check exits non-zero.

## Scaffold updates

Copier owns `.copier-answers.yml`; do not edit that provenance file by hand.
From a clean branch, check whether the scaffold source has a newer tagged release:

```bash
uvx --from copier==9.17.0 copier check-update --quiet
```

Exit status `0` means the repository is current, while exit status `2` means
an update is available. Any other status is an error. Apply the update
non-interactively from the recorded answers with Copier's three-way merge and
explicit inline conflicts:

```bash
just scaffold-update
```

If Copier cannot merge a local customization automatically, it leaves standard
inline conflict markers. Resolve every marker, then run the generated
repository's existing guard and full gate before committing:

```bash
uv run prek run check-merge-conflict --all-files
just check
```

## Coding-agent session evidence

Preserve a complete Claude Code or Codex CLI session for harness analysis:

```bash
# Codex: use a rollout, never ~/.codex/history.jsonl
just session-log ~/.codex/sessions/YYYY/MM/DD/rollout-SESSION.jsonl .agent-sessions/codex

# Claude: the matching session-stem/subagents tree is included recursively
just session-log ~/.claude/projects/PROJECT/SESSION.jsonl .agent-sessions/claude
```

To exercise the importer against private local logs, run `just session-e2e`.
This opt-in test chooses one primary transcript at random from each of five size
quantiles under `~/.claude/projects/` and `~/.codex/sessions/`; it skips an agent
when no matching local sessions exist. The standard quality gate excludes it so
routine tests never inspect personal logs unexpectedly.

The command auto-detects the agent; pass the optional third argument `claude`
or `codex` to require one format. It writes ATIF-v1.7, a tab-separated compact
profile, a chronological/hierarchical route, SHA-256 blobs, and a self-checking
manifest. Transformation is deterministic and performs no semantic analysis.
Raw JSONL is preserved byte-for-byte. The ignored .agent-sessions default may
contain sensitive prompts, source, and tool output; on POSIX it is written with
owner-only permissions. Other platforms still require an access-control check
before sharing it deliberately.
The implementation comes from a private Git dependency pinned to an immutable
commit; this repository keeps only a lazy compatibility facade. Standard
bootstrap, `just check`, and CI do not resolve it. Before either opt-in session
command, authenticate [`gh`](https://cli.github.com/) for the private repository
and run `gh auth setup-git`; no credential is stored in this repository.
`docs/adr/0003-agent-session-evidence.md` owns the format and invariants.

## Try the example application

```bash
uv run python -m {{ package }} --help
uv run python -m {{ package }} capabilities --limit 100
uv run python -m {{ package }} add "First item" --idempotency-key first-item
uv run python -m {{ package }} list --name-contains First --field name --quiet
uv run python -m {{ package }} list               # bounded JSON with continuation metadata
uv run python -m {{ package }} list --format human
```

Every command is registered in the closed catalog at
`src/{{ package }}/adapters/inbound/cli_catalog.py` and has one independent
case in `tests/contract/cli_contract_cases.py`. The quality gate compares those
sets exactly, requires cases for each input/filter/field/outcome declaration,
requires an ordinary-argument success even when structured input is additive,
and invokes every case as a detached real process. JSON is the
automation default; `--format human` is explicit. Ordinary arguments are the
primary input and every current command forbids stdin. Capabilities publishes
argument shapes, query controls, safety, retry preconditions, and recoverable
outcomes. Conditional idempotency atomically couples the item and replay
record across both shipped repositories; durable event delivery would require
an application outbox. See `docs/adr/0002-agent-native-cli-protocol.md` and
`docs/adr/0004-agent-input-retry-and-composition-contract.md` before changing
input paths, schema versions, exit classes, command kinds, bounds, outcomes,
or retry and safety policy.

